1) Scan for any unit clauses (none found) and pure literals (none found).  
2) Pick a literal (e.g., x1) and assign True; propagate this choice, simplifying clauses that become satisfied and removing x1 from others.  
3) If a clause becomes unit, propagate that new forced assignment. If any clause becomes empty, that is a conflict → backtrack and flip the previous choice of x1 to False; propagate again.  
4) Continue this process, systematically branching on unassigned literals, applying propagation each time, and backtracking whenever a conflict arises. Keep track of the number of branching decisions and the number of conflicts (backtracks).  
5) Eventually, this procedure detects that no complete satisfying assignment is possible under any branch, showing the formula is unsatisfiable.  

